the director of the National Museum of  Afghanistan summed up much of what we  are thinking about at the moment in  particular with the words a nation stays  alive when its culture stays alive and  what we're witnessing particularly in  the last few weeks is a systematic  attempt to destroy culture and redefine  national boundaries what I'm going to  now talk about are some case studies and  experiences and positive experiences of  working with the National Museum of  Afghanistan in over the last few years  in particular and to describe what can  be done perhaps in connection with other  countries looting is well has been  endemic in Afghanistan probably since  the late 60s is well documented through  the 70s declined during the period of  the Soviet occupation rose again  dramatically during the civil war in the  chaos that ensued when the museums were  also targeted fell under the Taliban  years it seems but was replaced by  annika iconoclastic destruction in the  last year of Taliban rule of a size and  scale very similar to what we're  witnessing today in Iraq and Syria  there were many attempts by particular  by private individuals to try and  safeguard and record Afghan cultural  heritage but official governmental  responses I think it has to be said were  generally very slow and late and really  followed the fall of the Taliban  government in 2001 in 2006 the icon red  list was finally launched in London  turning to a sister institution of this  on the occasion of an exhibition at the  British Museum in 2011 there was the  opportunity to sign an official  Memorandum of Understanding between the  relevant  directors of the British Museum in the  National Museum in Kabul and to  formalize our intent to work very  closely together on the issue of stolen  cultural heritage whether it be from  museums in Afghanistan or from  archaeological sites so I'll run through  three case studies because I know you've  only got a few minutes the first case  study revolved around a group of twenty  of the famous by Graham ivories lost  from the museum in Kabul during the  Civil War  disappeared onto the black market  tracked down by a private individual  reacquired for the museum in Kabul with  the blessing of that museum conserved  and analyzed by us at the Museum in  London and returned by us to Kabul and  the end of last year we were very  pleased to announce and present in Kabul  the full publication of the work we did  with our colleagues there the second  case study revolves around another  famous object the largest piece of  Gandharan sculpture from the museum in  Kabul which was taken one night from  open display there during the Civil War  DISA crew disappeared across borders  into a country that is not a signatory  of UNESCO into a private collection its  whereabouts was communicated through us  to the director of the National Museum  in Kabul who gave the go-ahead for it to  be acquired by any means necessary it  lay beyond the power of law or UNESCO  and so a private individual did  something which is often frowned on he  went and bought it because the owner  would not give it back and could not be  compelled to give it back he went and  bought it and here iam ported it into  Britain it was taken from the airport  straight to  Museum where as inspected by curators  from Kabul verified it still had its  Museum number on I had to say we  exhibited it temporarily in order to  maximize press attention on this event  and it is now back on display in Kabul  the third case study revolves around a  different type of problem which is a  much more large scale and much more  difficult problem and that's the looting  of archaeological sites which has been  endemic now in Afghanistan for decades  now over the years in the last decade  and a half thousands of objects which we  believe in in British Museum to  originate in Afghanistan have been  seized by UK border force with the  assistance of particular nuns team and  the Ottoman teks unit of the Met and the  HMRC  we've catalogued them and we have  returned them to Kabul is as to great  big consignments of objects this process  has involved a lot of people it's  involved a lot of organizations  including NGOs and the Red Cross and  it's involved the British Armed Forces  also in returning large quantities of  material in a safe way we've also been  very anxious to make sure that the  recording of those objects before  they're sent back is done to a  relatively high standard that we hand  back condition reports of those objects  and that we are fully transparent in  what has been sent back and this is  again from the wishes of Kabul who have  given us the green light to publish a  full catalogue of what we've sent so  that is my last closing slide showing  the Buddha from suraíh Kuja back on  display beautiful display in Kabul and  the press release on 5th of August 2012  when you can see some of the Bagram  ivories and looted  is going on display in Kabul so clearly  there are lessons to be learnt from  working together and I'm talking museum  to Museum organization to organization  across international boundaries and  involving private individuals and the  trade as well to identify catalog and  where possible repatriate pieces to  countries of origin thank you
 

